Let $\mathbb{R}^2$ (identified with $\mathbb{C}$) denote the plane. Two self-mappings $f, g : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ are said to be conjugate under a map $\varphi : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ if $\varphi$ is a bijection and $g = \varphi^{-1} \circ f \circ \varphi$. Give an intrinsic characterization of those self-mappings $f : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ that are conjugate to some complex polynomial $P(z) = a_n z^n + a_{n-1} z^{n-1} + \cdots + a_0$ (with $a_i \in \mathbb{C}$, $n \geq 0$) in each of the following three senses:
(a) conjugacy via a diffeomorphism $\varphi$ of the plane;
(b) conjugacy via a homeomorphism $\varphi$ of the plane;
(c) conjugacy via an arbitrary bijection $\varphi$ of the plane.
In each case, provide necessary and sufficient conditions on $f$ for such a conjugacy to exist.

